<ArticleLayout
  title="What the CAIGP Certification Actually Covers"
  description="A practitioner's honest breakdown of the Certified AI Governance Professional curriculum: what it teaches, what it misses, and how it applies in production."
  date="2025-03"
  maturity="evergreen"
  readTime="6 min"
  type="ai"
  tags={['CAIGP', 'Certification', 'Fundamentals', 'Compliance']}
>
  <p>
    As regulatory standards around artificial intelligence develop across jurisdictions, including the EU AI Act, 
    the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, and Singapore's Model AI Governance Framework, demand for 
    standardized governance credentials has grown rapidly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>Certified Artificial Intelligence Governance Professional (CAIGP)</strong> credential 
    represents one of the primary professional certifications connecting legal compliance, engineering practices, and risk management. 
    Here is an honest breakdown of the curriculum from a field practitioner.
  </p>

  <h2>Core Pillars of the Body of Knowledge</h2>

  <h3>1. AI Architecture and Technical Fundamentals</h3>
  <p>
    Understanding machine learning workflows (supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement learning, LLMs, diffusion models), 
    data pipelines, training processes, and where technical failures and biases occur across the lifecycle.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Global Regulatory Approaches</h3>
  <p>
    Review of major regulatory structures: risk-tiered requirements (such as the EU AI Act), voluntary consensus standards (such as NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001), 
    and sector-specific guidance in banking and healthcare.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Risk Assessments and Impact Auditing</h3>
  <p>
    Practical methods for running AI Impact Assessments (AIIA), assigning operational accountability, mapping vendor supply chain risks 
    (foundation model providers versus fine-tuned internal apps), and setting up continuous monitoring.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Translating Principles into Operational Controls</h3>
  <p>
    Turning broad corporate commitments like fairness, transparency, and accountability into documented, auditable operational controls.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the Certification Ends and Production Begins</h2>
  <p>
    The certification provides a solid legal and conceptual framework. In production, however, AI governance requires 
    practical operational discipline. Governance is not about checking compliance boxes. It is about building the organizational 
    readiness to delay an unstable launch, address unexpected model drift calmly, and set up clear channels for user feedback.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
